## Context
Harness text about long or external waits disagrees with itself and names one
runtime's tools in portable files. The principle to align on: never block a
turn on a foreground sleep, watch or poll loop; launch long or external work
with the runtime's background facility and a deadline; act when it exits; read
forge state once per step; where the runtime cannot wake the session, read the
result once at the next step.

Already done on main: the queued-merge wait (merge § Waiting for a queued
merge, commits ce2965be and 70b39b0a, PR #1550) and its wake-up condition
(PR #1552, open when this ticket was filed). Everything else is open.

Facts that shape the work (probed 2026-10-21, Claude Code 2.1.306, Codex
0.163.0, Pi 1.3.0): only Claude Code's `run_in_background` wakes the session
when the command exits. Codex background terminals run but never start a turn;
Pi has no background tool. A command bounded by `timeout` is the deadline on
every runtime.

## Evidence (redo at Imagine time)
`docs/discussions/2026-10-21-longrun-language-audit.md` (landed with this
ticket) lists contradictions C1 to C7, gaps G1 to G12, runtime leaks A1 to A9, 18 proposed
edits, a proposed hints list and its location. It read a tree 48 commits
behind main and was only partly re-checked after the rebase; its line numbers
and the merge-wait findings are stale. Rerun the audit against main when this
ticket moves to Imagine, before trusting any line in it.

## Parked alternative (not part of this ticket)
`docs/discussions/2026-10-21-await-external-design.md` (landed with this
ticket) describes a homegrown waiter (systemd transient unit, one recorded outcome, delivery by
live push, resume or pull). Parked, no code. It matters only if the aligned
instructions leave Codex or Pi unable to resume after a wait; reopen it then.
The Stopguard design (docs/2026-10-21-stopguard-design.md, tracker 1164) reads
the same wait registry for its rule 2 and will cite the "Waiting" paragraph this
ticket adds.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] The audit is redone against current main, and its contradictions, gaps
  and runtime leaks are listed with fresh file:line.
- [ ] `rules/workflow.md` carries a short Waiting paragraph stating the
  principle, and `rules/git.md` no longer says "wait for CI" without a
  mechanism. The resident rules stay within `RESIDENT_BUDGET` (trim, or a
  separate budget decision).
- [ ] One hints list, `RUNTIME: FUNCTIONNAME`, at a location chosen at Imagine
  time (the audit proposes `adapters/runtime-hints.md`); skills point to it and
  carry no runtime tool names.
- [ ] Skills with an unbounded or undescribed wait are aligned: the pre-PR
  gate, `make check` in lair, local CI on padme, headless seat launches,
  release audits, team-lead executors, raid and review-pr seats, trace-doctor.
  Each background command has a deadline.
- [ ] Fork polling is a named exception: one bounded tool call, not one call
  per check.
- [ ] The agnostic checker catches the runtime tool names it now misses, or the
  decision not to extend it is recorded.
- [ ] Waits on Codex and Pi are tested or the gap is stated: the Codex
  `functions.wait`, `notify()` and sub-agent completion paths were
  self-reported, not exercised.
- [ ] `make check` passes.
